questions.question:
Part of the loop after a first fix:

for (const call of calls) {
    try {
        results.push({ role: "tool", callId: call.id, name: call.name, ok: true, result: await tools.get(call.name)!.run(call.args) });
    } catch (err) {
        return { status: "failed", answer: "We couldn't process your request. A human will get back to you." };
    }
}

Trace of ticket #9131 with this loop:

turn 1  model -> call_1 list_orders {}
        tool  -> threw TimeoutError: orders API timed out after 5000 ms
        run   -> failed, fallback reply

The same ticket replayed with a scripted model, the error appended as { ok: false, error: { code: "tool_failed", message: "orders API timed out after 5000 ms" } }:

turn 2  model -> call_2 list_orders {}
        tool  -> ok [ord_88 paid, ord_89 authorization_pending]

Statement: "Ending the run when a tool throws is the safer design here, because the model has no way to use the result of a failed tool call."

Turn 3 of a run, and what the loop appended:

turn 3  model -> [call_3 get_order {"order_id":"ord_88"}, call_4 get_progress {"course_slug":42}]

transcript after turn 3:
  { role: "assistant", toolCalls: [call_3 get_order, call_4 get_progress] }
  { role: "tool", callId: "call_3", name: "get_order", ok: true, result: { id: "ord_88", status: "paid" } }

loop code for each call:
  const violations = tool.validate(call.args);
  if (violations.length > 0) continue;          // skip invalid calls

next model request -> 400 invalid_request: tool call call_4 has no matching tool result

Statement: "The request fails because call_4 was skipped when it failed validation; appending an invalid_arguments error for call_4 after call_3's result lets the run continue and tells the model what to fix."

